      <div type="clause" n="30.5">
        <head><ref type="lawCode" target="law:IICn">II Cn</ref> 30.5</head>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="30.5" corresp="#wit-g-a"><pb n="332" ed="#liebermann"/>⁊ gif he<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="25" target="#IICn_30-5.lnote.oe.25"/> þonn<hi rend="italic">e</hi> gyt mare wurc geworht hæbbe, þonne do man ut his eagan, ⁊ ceorfan of his nósu<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#IICn_30-5.lnote.oe.1-4"/> ⁊ his earan ⁊ þa uferan lippan oððon hine hættian, swa hwylc<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="2" target="#IICn_30-5.lnote.oe.2-4"/> þyssa swa man þonne<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="3" target="#IICn_30-5.lnote.oe.3-3"/> geræde, ða þe ðærto rædan<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="4" target="#IICn_30-5.lnote.oe.4"/> sceolon: swa man mæg styran ⁊ eac þære<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="5" target="#IICn_30-5.lnote.oe.5"/> sawle beorgan.<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="6" target="#IICn_30-5.lnote.oe.6"/></ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="30.5" corresp="#wit-b"><pb n="332" ed="#liebermann"/>⁊ gyf hit<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="2" target="#IICn_30-5.lnote.oe.2-2"/> ðonne gyt mare weorc geweorht<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="2" target="#IICn_30-5.lnote.oe.2-2"/> hæbbe, ðonne do man ut his eagan, ⁊ ceorfan of his nose ⁊ eáran ⁊ ða uferan lippan oððe hine hǽttian, swylc ðisra swa man wylc oððe ðonne gerǽde, ða ðe ðærto rædan sceolon: swa man [sceal<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#IICn_30-5.lnote.oe.1-2"/>] steoran ⁊ eac ðære saule beorgan.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="30.5" corresp="#wit-a"><pb n="332" ed="#liebermann"/>⁊ gif he þonn<hi rend="italic">e</hi> git<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="5" target="#IICn_30-5.lnote.oe.5-2"/> mare wurc geworoht<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#IICn_30-5.lnote.oe.1-5"/> hæbbe, þone<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#IICn_30-5.lnote.oe.1-5"/> do man ut his eagan, ⁊ ceorfan of his nase ⁊ earan ⁊ uferan lippe<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="†" target="#IICn_30-5.lnote.oe.dagger"/> oðð<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#IICn_30-5.lnote.oe.1-5"/> hine hettian, swa hwylc þissa swa man þonne geræde, þa þe þærto ræden<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#IICn_30-5.lnote.oe.1-5"/> sceolan: swa man mæg styran ⁊ eac þare sawle gebeorgan.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="30.5" corresp="#wit-q"><pb n="333" ed="#liebermann"/>Et si adhuc amplius peccauerit<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="14" target="#IICn_30-5.lnote.oe.14"/>, educantur<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="15" target="#IICn_30-5.lnote.oe.15"/> ei oculi et truncetur<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="16" target="#IICn_30-5.lnote.oe.16"/> ei nasus et aures et superlabrum<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#IICn_30-5.lnote.oe.1-3"/> aut decapilletur, quicquid horum consuluerint<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="2" target="#IICn_30-5.lnote.oe.2-3"/>, quorum tunc intererit; sic<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="3" target="#IICn_30-5.lnote.oe.3-2"/> corrigi poterit et animę prouideri.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="30.5" corresp="#wit-inst-cn"><pb n="333" ed="#liebermann"/>Quodsi adhuc maiora fecerit, eruantur sibi oculi, et auferatur sibi<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="20" target="#IICn_30-5.lnote.oe.20"/> nasus cum labro et aures aut corium capitis cum capillis, quod Angli dicunt behætian<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#IICn_30-5.lnote.oe.1-6"/>; sic possunt latrones corrigi et animę saluari.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="30.5" corresp="#wit-cons-cn"><pb n="333" ed="#liebermann"/>Quodsi maius expetit, oculi eius eruantur, et nasus curtetur, auresque cum superiore labro abscindantur, aut pilletur, quod horum illi decreuerunt [qui] consulere debent, quomodo possint et peccationem corrigere et anime illius parcere.</ab>
        <ab type="editionText" xml:lang="ang" source="#robertson" n="30.5" corresp="#wit-b"><pb n="190" ed="#robertson"/>⁊ gyf h[e]<ptr type="editorNote" n="9" target="#IICn_30-5.robnote.fn.9"/> ðonne gyt mare weorc geweorht hæbbe, ðonne do man ut his eagan ⁊ ceorfan of his nose ⁊ eáran ⁊ ða uferan lippan oððe hine hǽttian, swylc ðisr<ptr type="editorNote" n="10" target="#IICn_30-5.robnote.fn.10"/>a swa man wyle, oðð<ptr type="editorNote" n="11" target="#IICn_30-5.robnote.fn.11"/>e ðonne gerǽde ða ðe ðærto rædan sceolon; swa man sce<ptr type="editorNote" n="12" target="#IICn_30-5.robnote.fn.12"/>al steoran ⁊ eac ðære saule beorgan.</ab>
        <ab type="editionText" xml:lang="ang" source="#rabin-doml" corresp="#wit-g-a"><pb n="270" ed="#rabin-doml"/>And gif he þonne gyt mare wurc geworht hæbbe, þonne do man ut his eagan, and ceorfan of his nosu and his earan and þa uferan lippan oððon hine hættian, swa hwylc þyssa swa man þonne geræde, ða þe ðærto rædan sceolon; swa man mæg styran and eac þære sawle beorgan.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="de" source="#liebermann"><pb n="333" ed="#liebermann"/>Und wenn er dann noch grössere<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#IICn_30-5.lnote.de.1"/> Missethat verübt hat, dann reisse man seine Augen aus, und sie sollen seine Nase<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="2" target="#IICn_30-5.lnote.de.2"/> und seine Ohren und die Oberlippen abschneiden oder ihu scalpiren<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="3" target="#IICn_30-5.lnote.de.3"/>, was immer unter diesen [Strafen] man<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="4" target="#IICn_30-5.lnote.de.4"/> (will oder <hi rend="italic">B</hi>) dann beschliesst — [nämlich] die, welche darüber beschliessen sollen —: so<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="5" target="#IICn_30-5.lnote.de.5"/> kann [soll <hi rend="italic">B</hi>] man strafen und [doch] auch die Seele schonen.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude" ana="#fromGerman">And if he has then committed still greater wrong, let his eyes be put out, and let them cut off his nose and his ears and his upper lip, or scalp him, whichever of these [punishments] is then decided on (or wished, B) — [namely] by those who are to decide on it —: thus one can (must, B) punish and yet also spare the soul.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#robertson" corresp="#wit-b"><pb n="191" ed="#robertson"/>And if he has wrought still greater crime, he shall have his eyes put out and his nose and ears and upper lip cut off or his scalp removed<ptr type="editorNote" n="1" target="#IICn_30-5.robnote.n.1"/>, whichever of these penalties is desired or determined upon by those with whom rests the decision of the case; and thus punishment shall be inflicted, while, at the same time, the soul is preserved from injury.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#whitelock"><pb n="466" ed="#whitelock"/>And if, however, he has committed still further crimes, his eyes are to be put out and his nose and ears and upper lip cut off, or his scalp removed, whichever of these is then decreed by those with whom the decision rests; thus one can punish and at the same time preserve the soul.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#rabin-doml" corresp="#wit-g-a"><pb n="271" ed="#rabin-doml"/>And if he has committed an even greater crime, then his eyes shall be put out and his nose, ears, and upper lip cut off or his scalp taken off, whichever of these is decreed by the person with the authority to pass judgment; thus may a person be punished yet his soul be saved.</ab>
        <note type="comparison" source="#wormald-ppmel" n="93"><label>spells out more clearly</label> <ref target="clause:EGu_10">EGu 10</ref> <quote>Wulfstan’s ‘Edward and Guthrum’ tract spelled it out more clearly ... II Cn 30:5, EGu 10</quote></note>
        <note type="editorial">A's ‘uferan lippe’ is Middle English. In G, ‘ge’ was written above ‘beorgan’. Liebermann compares Northu 61 (?).; BR: added from Gesetze I p. 333, October 2026 (MS Cons); AY: corrected against Rabin p. 270 / 271, October 2026 (was “∅; ∅”, now “|; |”)</note>
