        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_30-5.lnote.oe.3-3" n="3" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">334</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">wyle oþþe ðone g. <hi rend="italic">Ld</hi> [<hi rend="italic">aus B</hi>]</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">wyle oþþe ðone g. Ld [from B]</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_30-5.lnote.oe.4" n="4" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">334</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">-den sceolan <hi rend="italic">Ld</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">-den sceolan Ld</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_30-5.lnote.oe.5" n="5" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">334</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">ðare <hi rend="italic">Ld</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">ðare Ld</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_30-5.lnote.oe.6" n="6" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">334</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">geb. <hi rend="italic">Ld; davor</hi> ge <hi rend="italic">übergeschr. G</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">geb. Ld; before it ge written above G</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_30-5.lnote.oe.5-2" n="5" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">332</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">verb. aus</hi> gig</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">corrected from gig</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_30-5.lnote.oe.1-5" n="1" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">332</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">so</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">sic</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_30-5.lnote.oe.dagger" n="†" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">334</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">Mittelenglisch</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Middle English</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_30-5.lnote.oe.20" n="20" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">333</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">fehlt Lb</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">lacking in Lb</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_30-5.lnote.oe.1-6" n="1" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">335</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">-ættie <hi rend="italic">nachträglich Cb;</hi> -hedian <hi rend="italic">S;</hi> bihedian <hi rend="italic">Lb</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">-ættie added later Cb; -hedian S; bihedian Lb</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IICn_30-5.lnote.de.1" n="1" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">206</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">3</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Nicht ‘weiter’ im Sinne von ‘zeitlich fernerhin <hi rend="spaced">nach</hi> dem Rückfalle’ (Schreuer <hi rend="italic">Verbrechenskonk</hi>. 165), wie vielleicht Q versteht</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Not ‘further’ in the sense ‘later in time, <hi rend="italic">after</hi> the relapse’ (Schreuer <hi rend="italic">Verbrechenskonk</hi>. 165), as Q perhaps understands it</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IICn_30-5.lnote.de.2" n="2" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">206</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">3</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">Vgl. Gl</hi> Ohr 2; Nase 2; Auge 4</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">Cf. Gl</hi> Ohr 2; Nase 2; Auge 4</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IICn_30-5.lnote.de.3" n="3" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">206</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">3</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">Ebd</hi>. Skalpieren. Eine Aldhelm-Glosse bietet: <hi rend="italic">decaluata: unhufed, behættod</hi>; ed. Napier <hi rend="italic">Old Engl. glosses</hi> p. 116. <hi rend="italic">Decalvare</hi> öfter bei festländ. Germanen. Ælfric übersetzt <hi rend="italic">cute capitis abstracta</hi> (II Mac. 7, 4) durch <hi rend="italic">behættian; Saints</hi> II 74. Grimms etymolog. Beziehung zu *<hi rend="italic">behedan, hydan</hi>, ‘enthäuten’ ist abzulehnen</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">Ibid</hi>. Skalpieren. An Aldhelm gloss offers: <hi rend="italic">decaluata: unhufed, behættod</hi>; ed. Napier <hi rend="italic">Old Engl. glosses</hi> p. 116. <hi rend="italic">Decalvare</hi> occurs frequently among the continental Germanic peoples. Ælfric translates <hi rend="italic">cute capitis abstracta</hi> (II Mac. 7, 4) by <hi rend="italic">behættian; Saints</hi> II 74. Grimm's etymological connection with *<hi rend="italic">behedan, hydan</hi>, ‘to flay’, is to be rejected</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IICn_30-5.lnote.de.4" n="4" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">207</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">1</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Das Gericht (so Maurer in <hi rend="italic">Kr. Übsch</hi>. III 58); <hi rend="italic">vgl. Gl</hi> d. 1h</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">The court (so Maurer in <hi rend="italic">Kr. Übsch</hi>. III 58); <hi rend="italic">cf.</hi> that word in <hi rend="italic">Gl</hi> 1h</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IICn_30-5.lnote.de.5" n="5" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">207</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">1</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Mit Q Inst interpungier ich <hi rend="spaced">vór</hi> letztem <hi rend="italic">swa</hi>; bei C's <hi rend="italic">quomodo</hi>, abhängig von <hi rend="italic">rædan</hi>, = Schmid's ‘wie’, würde man Opt. statt <hi rend="italic">mæg</hi> erwarten.—Sachlich und wörtlich ebenso <hi rend="italic">o</hi>. 2, 1³</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">With Q Inst I punctuate <hi rend="italic">before</hi> the last <hi rend="italic">swa</hi>; with C's <hi rend="italic">quomodo</hi>, dependent on <hi rend="italic">rædan</hi>, = Schmid's ‘how’, one would expect an optative instead of <hi rend="italic">mæg</hi>.—The same in substance and wording <hi rend="italic">above</hi> 2, 1³</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="endnote" xml:id="IICn_30-5.robnote.n.1" n="1" source="#robertson"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">356</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">Cf. III Edm. 4; <hi rend="italic">Sax Chr.</hi>, ann. 1036 C.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="IICn_30-5.robnote.fn.9" n="9" source="#robertson"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">190</biblScope></bibl><label>h[e</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en"><hi rend="italic">hit</hi> B, Ld.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="IICn_30-5.robnote.fn.10" n="10" source="#robertson"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">190</biblScope></bibl><label>ðisra</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en"><hi rend="italic">swa hwylc þyssa</hi> (<hi rend="italic">þissa</hi>) G, A; <hi rend="italic">-ce ðissa</hi> Ld.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="IICn_30-5.robnote.fn.11" n="11" source="#robertson"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">190</biblScope></bibl><label>oððe</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en"><hi rend="italic">w. o.</hi> om. G, A.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="IICn_30-5.robnote.fn.12" n="12" source="#robertson"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">190</biblScope></bibl><label>sceal</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en"><hi rend="italic">sceal</hi> written above in 16th cent. in B; <hi rend="italic">mæg</hi> G, A, Ld.</seg></note>
